<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ismail al-Jazari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1136–1206</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ismail al-Jazari was an engineer and inventor who served the Artuqid rulers of Diyarbakır in upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> for about 25 years. Around 1206 he completed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-knowledge-of-ingenious-mechanical-devices">Book of Knowledge of Ingenious Mechanical Devices</a>, describing water clocks, automata, pumps and other machines with detailed drawings. He is one of the best-known engineers of the medieval Islamic world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ismail-al-jazari.jpg" alt="Ismail al-Jazari" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Al-Jazari · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-jazari_elephant_clock.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
